// The mark that says a function's answer is fetched.

import ir "sngl:internal/ir"

// Marks a function whose answer is fetched, keyed and cached.
//
// The function returns a `Value<T>` and its body is the fetch. The compiler
// rewrites it to a lookup: one box per argument tuple, started on first read,
// re-fetched when an argument changes, and shared by every part of the program
// asking the same question. So two calls with equal arguments are one box and
// one fetch, and a call whose arguments differ is a different box.
//
// The mark exists because the return type alone cannot say this. A `Value<T>` is
// a value like any other and a function may hand one back without any of the
// above — a wrapper forwarding another query's answer, or one of the three
// constructors. Marking the function is what separates "returns a box" from
// "is a query", and an unmarked function returning a `Value` is the former:
// well defined, and never silently half-lowered.
//
// A query's arguments become its key, so each must be encodable as one. That
// rules out a function, a component and another `Value`; scalars, strings,
// enums, units and collections of those are all fine. The rule reaches the
// arguments and not the fetched type — what comes *back* is never part of a key.
//
// Reactivity needs nothing declared. A prop reading `users(page)` mentions
// `page`, so the ordinary dependency walk re-evaluates it when `page` changes;
// the re-evaluation looks up a different key, and a key with no fetch behind it
// starts one. That is why a key may be a loop variable or any other derived
// value, none of which a program could have named in advance.
func query() ir.Macro {}
